Fundamental laws of nature do more than summarize observations: within their domain of validity, they can predict new phenomena.The electromagnetic laws formulated by Maxwell (麦克斯韦) are an example.If each law merely repeated the observations on which it was based, a separate rule would be needed for every phenomenon.Scientific laws are valuable because connected principles explain many phenomena and lead to predictions that can be tested.Maxwell's (麦克斯韦) electromagnetic field laws lead to many important conclusions.One is that **changes in electromagnetic interaction propagate at a finite speed**, rather than reaching all of space instantaneously.In the historical model of instantaneous action at a distance, a change in one charge's position immediately changes the Coulomb force on another charge.The charges are imagined to respond directly across the intervening vacuum, with no propagation time.
Coulomb's law applies to electrostatic situations.It cannot be applied without qualification to moving charges to conclude that real changes in interaction are transmitted instantaneously.In Maxwell's (麦克斯韦) field theory, motion of a charge changes the nearby electromagnetic field, and the disturbance propagates outward.Time-varying electric and magnetic fields are linked by the electromagnetic field laws.
A step-by-step diagram helps illustrate this connection.The actual electric and magnetic fields evolve together in space and time;
they are not separate objects created one after another instantaneously.Figure 103 is a schematic of these field relationships.The changing magnetic field is represented by field lines labeled $B$, and the associated circulating electric field by lines labeled $E$.Changes in the electric field are in turn linked to the magnetic field labeled $B_1$.
The curves and arrows show field lines and their directional relationships.They are not exact snapshots of a particular wave at successive times, and they supply no numerical field strengths or propagation distances.Figure 103The original textbook uses opposing directions of old and newly induced fields in overlapping regions to illustrate the disturbance advancing into new regions of space.In the relationship shown, the magnetic induction vectors $\vec{B}$ and $\vec{B}_1$ point in opposite directions in the same region;the directional relationship between $\vec{E}$ and $\vec{E}_1$ is similar.
Opposite directions do not mean that the two fields cancel completely in every situation.The actual fields must be solved together using the electromagnetic field laws;the schematic does not imply that an old field always disappears and is replaced by an equally strong new field.These directional relationships are connected to the laws discussed in Section 50.The circulating electric field $E$ associated with a changing magnetic field, and the magnetic field $B$ associated with a changing electric field, cannot have their respective laws replaced arbitrarily by the same direction rule.The relative signs of the two field relationships matter.Using incorrect signs can lead to an unphysical prediction of field energy growing without a source, contrary to energy conservation.This does not mean that actual electromagnetic fields grow without limit in one region or propagate uniformly in every direction under all conditions.A change in a charge's position produces an electromagnetic field disturbance.As the disturbance propagates outward, it changes the field that existed around the charge before the motion.The force on another charge changes in response when the disturbance reaches it, not at the same instant as the first charge moves.Maxwell's (麦克斯韦) field theory describes this finite-speed propagation;
it does not require electromagnetic disturbances to travel through a mechanical medium.Maxwell (麦克斯韦) derived from the electromagnetic field laws that the speed of electromagnetic disturbances in vacuum equals the speed of light in vacuum.The vacuum condition is essential;
the same speed cannot be assumed for every material medium.This theoretical prediction revealed an important connection between electromagnetic fields and light.It supports studying the field as a physical entity with observable effects, but mathematical derivation alone does not replace experimental testing.

